use @piton/belay

from ../lib/PitonSkillBase import PitonSkillBase

export skill CruxSpecDistill extends PitonSkillBase:
    description:
        Distils everything a Piton specification says about one thing the
        user names, such as a colour, a control, a rule, a behaviour, or
        a whole skill, into one short prompt a builder agent can act on.
    useWhen:
        the user names a part of the product, however small, and wants a
        distilled summary of what the specification says about it, or a
        prompt to build, refactor, or check that part
    prompt:
        You answer one request: here is one thing in the product, tell
        me what the specification says about it, distilled. The user
        names the thing in their own words, such as the right sidebar
        border colour, the empty state of the search results, how the
        header behaves on a Thai page, or the develop skill. The
        specification usually says something about it in more than one
        place. A component states its own value, a visual language
        defines the palette that value comes from, a scoped instruction
        sets rules for every component in that folder, and a project
        invariant rules out whole approaches. Over time these drift
        apart from each other and from the code. You gather all of them
        and distil them into one prompt, so the user can hand it to a
        builder and bring that one thing back in line.

        The user wants the distilled result, not a map of where things
        are. Do not show them a list of anchors, files, or slice
        targets to approve. Find, read, and distil, then give the
        result.


    findingTheThing:
        Work out what the user named. Find the anchor or property that
        defines it most directly, searching the specification source by
        name and by content. Then search outward for three kinds of spec.

        - What it depends on. The values, tokens, and anchors the target reads, embeds, extends, or references. piton slice on the target follows these for you.
        - What governs it. Anything elsewhere that constrains the target without the target naming it, such as a palette or design token set, a visual language, a scoped instruction for the code folder the thing lives in, a parent component or layout, a project invariant, or an accessibility, localisation, or theming rule. Search for the target's name, its category, and the words those rules use, and use piton reach to see what connects to it.
        - Where it shows up. Other anchors that state, reuse, or override the same value, including the same thing described under a different name.

        Read each one with piton slice, from the project directory, so
        you read what the compiler resolves rather than guessing at
        inheritance. Use only what says something a builder must obey
        for this thing. Never include something merely because it is
        nearby, imported, or reachable. Leave out framework definitions,
        such as Belay's own construct anchors, unless they constrain the
        thing. Leave out text that only says how the build reaches the
        thing, or that only mentions it in passing.

        Ask one short question only when the name could mean two
        different things and the answer changes the result, or when it
        matches nothing. Otherwise go straight to the result.

    distilling:
        Distil means condense without losing meaning. Rewrite the
        specification's content in fewer, plainer words, grouped by
        topic rather than by file or anchor. Keep every requirement,
        value, limit, default, and prohibition. Keep exact values
        exactly, such as colours, sizes, names, counts, and quoted
        strings. Drop repetition, rationale that does not change what
        to do, and examples whose point is already stated.

        Never add a requirement, fill a gap, resolve a contradiction, or
        soften a rule. Keep the strength of each rule, so never and must
        stay never and must, and should stays should. If two parts of
        the specification disagree, say so inside the result where it
        matters, naming both values, so the builder stops rather than
        picks one.

        Write for a builder working in the code. When the user names a
        job, open with one line stating it. Build means implement the
        thing where it is missing. Refactor means restructure the code
        so it follows the specification in one clear place, without
        changing what it does. Check means compare the code with the
        specification and fix every difference. When the user names no
        job, give only the distilled specification, with no task
        framing. When the project uses Belay with a codeRoot and the
        thing lives in code, name the code folders or files to start
        from, and only paths that exist.

        Use headings and short lists so the result is easy to scan. Do
        not cite .pi file paths, anchor paths, or compiled output paths.
        The result must stand on its own, so a builder with no access to
        the specification can act on it.

    output:
        Print the result in chat inside one fenced block, using a fence
        longer than any fence inside it, so the user can copy it whole.
        Do not write it to a file unless the user asks. After the block,
        add at most a few lines: contradictions you found, and anything
        a builder would need that the specification does not say.

        This skill is read-only. Never modify the specification or the
        code, run piton build, or write generated output.
